\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The conditional coefficients and positive mass}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For the \vocabk{first-loop observables}{fdip-00ZO},
assume #{m\ne0} and #{(m^2-s)^2-t\ne0}, with
#{s,t} from \ref{fdip-00ZD}. At a fixed connection,
the normalized paired coefficients of the trace and
#{P} are respectively
##{4\operatorname{Re}U,\qquad
-4\operatorname{Re}U\,
\frac{m(m^2-s)}{(m^2-s)^2-t}.}
At #{m=5}, every determinant and #{Z(5)} is strictly
positive, and all the fixed-connection and summed
normalizations are defined over #{\mathbb Q}.}

\proof{
\p{The trace observable is scalar, so its paired
coefficient is #{(\trace_F L_U)\det K_{U,V}(m)}.
Use \ref{fdip-00ZL} and divide by the nonzero
determinant. For the barred-first pair,
\ref{fdip-00WG} gives exactly
##{-\trace_F\bigl(L_U\pr_0K_{U,V}(m)^{-1}\iota_0\bigr).}
The degree-zero inverse of \ref{fdip-00ZF} is a scalar
multiple of #{\bbI_F}; its scalar and
#{\trace_F L_U=4\operatorname{Re}U} give the second
formula. This trace remains on #{F}, with the inclusion
and projection as displayed.}

\p{The table in \ref{fdip-00ZK} gives #{0\le s\le8}
and #{0\le t\le4}. Therefore
##{(25-s)^2-t\ge17^2-4=285>0.}
By \ref{fdip-00ZD}, each determinant is at least
#{285^4}. Summing the #{576} positive rational values
proves #{Z(5)>0}. In a field, each nonzero value is a
unit, so all the specified divisions are allowed.}
}
